<h1>Who audits your site</h1>
<p class="lede">I'm María Pía Peña Foissac, and I do the audits at tuaccesible.</p>

<div class="prose">
  <img class="portrait" src="/img/maria-pia.jpg" width="1458" height="1534" alt="">

  <p>I've used a screen reader for more than thirteen years. It isn't a tool I open in order to work: it's my primary way of getting to anything digital, every day, inside and outside this consultancy. When I test your product with NVDA, I'm using it the same way I use everything else.</p>
  <p>I work with NVDA, JAWS, Windows Narrator, and TalkBack on Android, and with keyboard-only navigation.</p>

  <h2>What I audit, and since when</h2>
  <p>I've audited accessibility professionally since 2021: more than four years evaluating websites, mobile apps, documents, and desktop software against WCAG 2.2, EN 301 549, and the European Accessibility Act.</p>
  <p>I write accessibility review reports with findings prioritized by severity, accessibility statements, and cognitive accessibility recommendations. My background is in software QA. That is why a finding reaches your team with steps to reproduce it and an acceptance criterion, not as a loose observation.</p>

  <h2>I also teach</h2>
  <p>I teach digital accessibility at the DALAT Foundation, and I delivered the accessibility module for XAcademy. Teaching forced me to explain technical barriers to people who don't write code, and that changed how I write reports: if a content owner can't understand the finding, the finding doesn't get fixed.</p>

  <h2>Certifications</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A11Y Development Knowledge Badge — IAAP, January 2026. Awarded for passing their accessibility knowledge test for developers with a grade above 85%. <a href="https://www.credly.com/badges/b1386a4d-61cc-4c64-a4c4-a399475a6e42">Verify on Credly</a>.</li>
    <li>Digital Accessibility Specialist, WCAG 2.2 Level AA — <span lang="es">Instituto de Accesibilidad</span> and DALAT, certified by <span lang="es">Universidad Nacional de Tres de Febrero</span>. October 2025.</li>
    <li>ARIA from Scratch for Developers and Designers — WeAAAre, July 2025.</li>
    <li>Digital Accessibility — Universidad Tecnológica Nacional, 2020.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Your report goes through two readings</h2>
  <p>I do the auditing. The report, no. Before it goes out, another person at tuaccesible reads it, checks the prioritization and argues with the criteria I applied. We argue for real: sometimes I drop a finding from critical to high because I can't defend it. That is the point. What reaches your team has already survived an internal argument.</p>
</div>

<div class="prose">
  <h2>Let's talk</h2>
  <p>If you have a product to audit, tell me where it stands and on what timeline. I'll tell you where to start.</p>
